    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hi, i'm studying phonetics and i'm a bit confused. can you explain the difference between voiced and unvoiced alveolar fricatives? specifically the difference between /s/ and /z/ sounds in terms of what the vocal cords are doing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The key difference between /s/ (voiceless) and /z/ (voiced) lies entirely in the vibration of your vocal cords during the sounds' production. For /z/, your vocal cords are vibrating when you force air through the narrow groove in your tongue called the alveolar ridge, creating a buzzing quality. In contrast, for /s/, your vocal cords remain open and silent while you push air through the same alveolar groove, resulting in a sharper, breathier sound without that vibrating hum. Interestingly, you can often hear this distinction by simply singing the word \"buzz\" versus \"fees\"—the humming sensation in \"buzz\" confirms the cords are working for /z/.",
