    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. so for the /s/ sound, since there's no vocal cord vibration, is it just pure air turbulence? and is there a way to physically feel the difference in the throat while saying them?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For the /s/ sound, airflow creates significant alveolar turbulence due to the narrow constriction, but there is no acoustic energy being added by the vocal cords. While the vocal thrill is absent in the throat for /s/, sensitive individuals might still feel a slight breathiness or lack of the distinct vibration that they significantly feel for /z/. This tactile difference makes it surprisingly difficult to differentiate relying solely on the source (vocal cords) versus the place of constriction if you are not listening carefully, as both /s/ and /z/ share identical articulation points. Ultimately, /s/ relies on noise generated from the turbulence alone, whereas /z/ combines that same turbulence with a harmonic frequency from the vibrating cords.",
